  <h2>The ponies</h2>
  <p>
    The club’s first pony, Nongshaba, was bought in 1996 with money put together by its executive members, and
    was ridden by Th. Kaoba. By 2018 the club had 80 horses.
  </p>
  <Todo>How many horses the club keeps today.</Todo>

  <h2>Riders of the future</h2>
  <p>
    The club brings young people to the horse. It has given riding classes to students of Mega Manipur School
    (2013–14), Assam Rifles Public School (2015–16) and Sishu Nistha Niketan (2017), and in 2024 ran a seven-day
    riding course with the Ethno Heritage Council (HERICOUN). Under Th. Kaoba, the club’s young members also
    learn to make saddles, whips and tack, and are coached in riding, arambai and pana kangjei.
  </p>
  <p>
    The club takes polo beyond the tournament ground too, organising exhibition games in rural Manipur with its
    own players, ponies and equipment.
  </p>

  <h2>Horses in Manipur’s cultural life</h2>
  <p>
    The club’s riders and horses are invited to take part in cultural and religious occasions across Manipur,
    among them Kwak Tanba, Mera Houchongba, Lai Haraoba, pana kangjei and arambai.
  </p>
